{"attack": true, "case": "The target conflates 'preference for higher empirical content' (a purely logical, non-inductive property) with 'preference for better-corroborated theories' (which inherently weights past test-survival). Higher empirical content alone, considered a priori, maximizes exposure to falsification. But preferring a theory BECAUSE it has already survived tests (i.e., is better corroborated) treats past performance as relevant to present methodological choice — which is an inductive inference the Popperian framework cannot license. The mechanism's justification that this is 'the only way to learn faster' presupposes that past corroboration predicts future epistemic fruitfulness, smuggling in exactly the expectation that 'the future will resemble the past' that the first countercondition says would be fatal. The defense thus collapses on its own declared counterconditions.", "premise": "The claim's subject is preference for better-CORROBORATED theories, not merely higher-content theories. Corroboration is defined by a theory's track record of surviving tests, so any preference grounded in corroboration as opposed to bare logical content necessarily weights past performance.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "cd3ca5fef732e6786486dde4fbdb985dad2460e082a09fbe759a376263881266", "quote": "The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a non-inductive, methodological rule of 'critical rationality' that maximizes exposure to falsification, not probability of truth."}, {"block": "cd3ca5fef732e6786486dde4fbdb985dad2460e082a09fbe759a376263881266", "quote": "Choosing the theory with higher empirical content (and thus higher corroborability) is the only way to learn faster, even if it doesn't make the theory more likely to be true."}], "counterexample": ["Two theories T1 and T2 with equal empirical content, where T1 has been corroborated and T2 has not yet been tested. On the target's stated grounds (maximizing exposure to falsification, higher content = faster learning), there is no non-inductive reason to prefer T1 over T2 — yet the target's claim is specifically about preferring the better-corroborated theory. Any preference for T1 here must invoke T1's past survival of tests as relevant, which is inductive."], "successor_question": "Can the target articulate a non-inductive reason to prefer a better-corroborated theory over an equally content-rich but untested one, without invoking the past test-performance as a guide to future epistemic utility?"}